WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:06.943
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.943 --> 00:00:08.440
I'm Saad Omer, I'm the director

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.720
of Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.170
In addition to YIGH, we are
indebted to the MacMillan Center

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.570
at Yale for co-sponsoring today's event.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.450
It's an honor for me to
introduce today's guest,

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:24.600
U.S. Senator Chris Murphy.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:28.640
Senator Murphy has been a
strong voice in the Senate

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.590
fighting for job creation,
affordable healthcare,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.610
education, sensible gun laws,

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.900
and a forward-looking foreign policy,

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.390
including the, and of particular

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.490
interest to us, global health.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:45.750
As a member of the Foreign
Relations Committee,

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:49.530
the Health, Education, Labor
and Pensions committee,

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.410
appropriately sort of abbreviated often

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:57.410
as the HELP committee and
the appropriations committee,

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:01.080
Senator Murphy has played a key role

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.780
and continues to play a
key role in policymaking

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.580
around critical global health issues.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.210
For example, he has introduced

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:13.210
the Global Health Security
and Diplomacy Act, GHSDA,

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.820
a legislation that is geared
towards better detection,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.130
deterrence, and containment of
infectious disease outbreaks

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.130
overseas before they
become global pandemics.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.980
As we will learn more today,

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.420
he was an early champion
for a very robust response

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.550
to the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:41.550
And he has also advocated
for a range of issues

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.040
in global health, including primary care,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.393
tuberculosis, and many other issues.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:50.820
We intentionally,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:54.710
we initially had the intention
to have an hour-long program.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.010
However, given the
deliberations that are happening

00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.840
in Congress around budget
reconciliation and other issues,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.180
i.e. Senator Murphy's day job,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.543
our conversation today
will be around 30 minutes.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:10.030
While we won't be able to take
audience's questions today

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:13.353
because of that change in time,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.820
I'm looking forward to a great
discussion, nevertheless.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:18.860
Welcome, Senator Murphy.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.060
We are so pleased to
have you with us today.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.240
- Dr. Omer, thank you very much.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.500
Appreciate you accommodating
my schedule here.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.430
It is a very busy week in the Senate,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:34.160
but this is still part of my day job,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.640
working not only on continuing to improve

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.100
the United States' response to COVID,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.580
putting ourselves back in a
leadership position globally

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.760
to help other nations respond,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.960
but also to communicate
what we're working on

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.180
to people in Connecticut
and around the country.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.960
So grateful for this opportunity

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.810
and looking forward to the conversation.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.060
- My pleasure.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.160
So to get us started,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.140
can you give us a sort
of a big picture view

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.060
of how the U.S. Congress
responded to the pandemic

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.620
and how it has evolved,

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.133
this response has evolved
since the early days of 2020?

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.650
- That's a important question

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.660
with a probably hour-long answer.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.700
And so I'll give you the
very abbreviated version.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.920
As you mentioned, I was
one of the earliest critics

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.230
of the Trump administration's
laissez-faire policy

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.000
towards coronavirus early on.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.410
As we all remember,
they were making claims

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.580
that it would be dispensed
within a week or two,

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:33.480
and put essentially all of
their eggs in one basket.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.920
That was the travel restriction basket,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.820
instead of requesting early
emergency funding from Congress

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.370
in order to start building
a robust supply chain

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.100
and training local hospital
and public health staff

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.570
around how to deal with early infections

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:55.240
and the early need to track and trace.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.860
They put in place what
ended up being affectless

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.180
set of travel restrictions

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:03.960
that ultimately was easily
pierced by the virus.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.010
Congress and Washington
has a deserved reputation

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.810
for being defined by
gridlock and stalemate.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.483
I will say 2020, an election year,

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:18.550
was a time of pretty incredible
bipartisan compromise.

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:21.130
On six different occasions,

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.470
five, six, seven and a half
a dozen separate occasions,

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.730
Congress came together,
Republicans and Democrats,

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:28.610
to pass significant bipartisan legislation

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.400
to address the coronavirus crisis.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.810
President Trump didn't lead.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.230
Republicans and Democrats in Congress led.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:39.720
And so the reason today that
you have small businesses

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:40.590
that are still standing,

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.900
the reason why schools
were able to reopen,

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:45.190
that's because Congress passed legislation

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.680
from the CARES Act through
to the December legislation

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.020
with both parties on board
to respond to the crisis.

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:54.980
Not coincidental to the
election of Joe Biden,

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:58.740
politics started to
creep into the question

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.930
of how the political class
talked about coronavirus.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.330
We were largely together through 2020.

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:05.250
But all of a sudden,

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.040
when the Democrat came
into the White House,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.610
Republicans started to raise new issues

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.030
about vaccine policy and mask wearing.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.210
And now, unfortunately,
many parts of the country

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.640
are divided along political lines

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.090
as to whether we should
follow a doctor's advice.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.520
And that is a failing
of our political system.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.340
In Connecticut, I think
we've been lucky that, well,

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.470
you still find politics.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:30.970
There's still a crowd that doesn't want

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.570
our kids wearing masks in school.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:34.743
Large Republicans I think in Connecticut

00:05:34.743 --> 00:05:39.660
have been at the table,
responsive, thoughtful.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.410
And I think that's part of
the reason why Connecticut

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.990
leads the pack nationally when
it comes to vaccination rates

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:47.180
and remains at the bottom of the pack

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.060
when it comes to infection rates.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:53.440
So Congress, I would argue,
had a pretty good 2020,

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.460
has not had a very good 2021

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.910
in terms of bipartisan
cooperation on COVID.

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.070
And as we'll talk about, my
hope is that we recognize

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.960
that we have to walk and
chew gum at the same time.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.190
We've gotta protect ourselves

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.110
against the current strains of COVID

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:13.720
while standing up a global infrastructure

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:17.650
to identify new strains
and future pathogens.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:19.250
And that's a lot of the
work that I'm engaged in

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.230
on the Foreign Relations committee.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.380
And frankly, Dr. Omer,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.920
we've been very grateful to
have your advice and counsel

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.050
on many of the pieces of legislation

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:30.540
that we've been working on here.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.410
I'm grateful for our personal partnership.

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.850
- My pleasure, and thanks
for highlighting that

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.700
in democracies, these
levels of initiatives

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.050
and response to crises
are inevitably political,

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.910
but they don't have to be partisan.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:48.340
And I think we are all served with,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:51.300
by sort of broad
engagement on some of these

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.780
major policy policy issues.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.960
And you have also been
a champion of efforts

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.890
to reduce the impact of misinformation

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.520
and disinformation during the pandemic,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.890
but broadly around health as well.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:09.590
What do you think we can
specifically do in this area

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.823
of reducing the impact of
misinformation and disinformation?

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.900
- As we all know, the
difficulty of getting

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:20.700
square information exists
beyond the health realm.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.470
I was, two weeks ago,
engaging in my annual walk

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.303
across the state.

00:07:26.303 --> 00:07:27.420
I take about a week every year,

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.240
and I walk from one end
of the state to the other.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.110
And I was walking through Middletown,

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.520
stopped to see an older
gentleman who was sitting

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.515
on a bench, I think waiting for a bus.

00:07:36.515 --> 00:07:38.660
And we got in a really
fascinating conversation.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.097
He was a Trump voter in 2016.

00:07:41.097 --> 00:07:43.440
He was a Biden voter in 2020.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.490
He's not particularly
partisan or political,

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:47.200
but his biggest worry

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.980
was where he should get good information.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:52.790
He doesn't trust any
information sources these days.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.870
He misses the time when there were only

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:58.920
three news broadcasts,
when he thought that news

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.350
was a public contribution of the networks

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.120
rather than a source of profit.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.450
And so this is a question

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.300
that a lot of Americans are having.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.310
Of course, when it comes
to health misinformation,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.520
it's deadly, right?

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.600
You get the wrong
information about politics.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.970
Maybe that causes you to
cast ill-informed vote.

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:19.460
You get the wrong information
about your health,

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:20.750
you may not survive.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.850
There's a Pew Center study suggesting that

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.620
about half of the American public reports

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.810
having consumed some
amount of misinformation,

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:33.870
fraudulent health information online,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.960
and all of us who've run
into an aunt or an uncle

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.300
or a friend who tells us that
you're more likely to die

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.633
if you get the vaccine,

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.830
we know how easy it is to
pick up that information.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:48.280
So I really think it's
time for a big investment

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.470
in health literacy.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:50.990
And I don't think this is a particularly

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.070
complicated endeavor because, you know,

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:56.870
there are a handful of
very trusted sources,

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:59.780
both public sector and
private sector sources.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:03.170
And engaging in a public relations effort

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:07.490
to just generally try to explain to people

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:11.230
which sources they can trust
and which sources they can't,

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:13.490
I think that that would
be really constructive.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:15.590
So I'm building legislation right now

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:17.740
that I've consulted with you, Dr. Omer on.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:20.550
I'm trying to win some
bipartisan support for it

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:24.280
that would push this
public information campaign

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:27.970
around where to get your
health information from.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.260
And we also, of course,

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:30.490
we're in a constant dialogue

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:34.350
with these social media outlets.

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.240
Won't surprise you that
Senator Blumenthal and I

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.700
are not satisfied with the degree

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:42.330
to which the big platforms
like Google or Facebook

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.350
have policed misinformation.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:47.030
I mean, I'm watching
Facebook ads on TV right now

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.260
in which they're basically defending

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:52.770
their right to put lies on the internet.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.540
And I think that's just
devastating for democracy,

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.893
but also devastating for
our COVID relief efforts.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.830
- So switching a little
bit to sort of last year

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:05.940
and combining a couple
of questions together

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.550
in the sense that last year,
as part of our response,

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:10.340
the U.S. response, there
were a lot of efforts

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:14.480
to push for a utilization of
the Defense Production Act

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.280
and some of the other
interventions to improve,

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.210
not only just domestic PPE supply,

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:21.970
personal protective equipment supply,

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.860
as well as make sure that
our allies around the world

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:29.820
had PPE and therapeutics to
reduce the spread of COVID-19.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:31.690
What did those efforts look like,

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.070
and how should we think differently

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.340
about our supply chains going forward?

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:39.130
And if you wanna elaborate
how you and other members

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.910
are approaching through broader
response to the pandemic,

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:44.050
perhaps you might wanna
combine that as well.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:46.730
But I'm interested in the context

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.420
of the Defense Production Act

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:52.410
and some of the sequelae
of some of those events.

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:53.440
- Well, first of all,

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.950
utilization of the Defense Production Act

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.610
for pandemic response is very much like

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:03.160
trying to take a square peg
and fit it in a round hole.

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:07.270
So, well, I have been amongst
the most vocal proponents

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.790
of utilizing the DPA.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:11.120
We just have to remember,

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:16.120
the DPA is a sort of war time initiative

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:17.950
to give the president of United States

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:21.200
the ability to commandeer
industrial resources

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.850
in order to make airplanes, right?

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.030
Or whatever you need in order
to fight a foreign enemy.

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:32.030
It was not designed to
produce masks or syringes.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:37.870
And so now that we are
getting to the point

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:42.073
where we can have some
20/20 vision on 2020,

00:11:43.348 --> 00:11:45.340
we should be thinking about rewriting

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:46.830
a version of the DPA,

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.800
specifically tailored for health crises.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.200
One of the things we found in 2020

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.490
was that the utilization
of the DPA was a mess,

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:54.970
largely because Donald Trump was a mess.

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:57.130
He put it in the hands of his son-in-law,

00:11:57.130 --> 00:11:59.690
who had no experience
running big supply chains,

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.680
but also because it inherently asks

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:04.100
the Department of Defense
to take a primary role.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.220
The Department of
Defense has no experience

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:07.610
in building these kinds of supply chains.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:10.370
And as we learned, the
agencies that should

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:14.520
have that kind of
expertise, CDC, NIH and HHS

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.980
also weren't very good at it.

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.430
So we've gotta sort of
build a permanent capacity

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.490
inside the federal government

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.170
to manage medical supply chains.

00:12:24.170 --> 00:12:26.800
And yes, we need to work with our allies

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.480
in order to build the permanent ability

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.860
to produce the most important equipment,

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:35.630
the most important supplies necessary.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:37.159
I think we've just gotta make a decision

00:12:37.159 --> 00:12:39.170
that when it comes to certain items,

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.950
we can't be reliant on
a Chinese supply chain.

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:47.100
That doesn't mean that
we have to be constantly

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.810
making gauze or syringes.

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:52.760
It means that we have to
have the plus up capacity,

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.640
the surge capacity in the United States

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:59.140
and our ally structure in
order to meet that demand

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:00.640
should another pandemic occur.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:02.453
- Wonderful.

00:13:02.453 --> 00:13:05.100
So I think there will be hopefully

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:08.630
a lot of sort of policy
changes, some introspection,

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:12.590
and hopefully some legislation
and policy interventions.

00:13:12.590 --> 00:13:17.050
Not all of them will be
legislative solutions.

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:18.790
How do we ensure that any policy changes

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:22.610
that are made to strengthen
our pandemic preparedness

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:25.230
are maintained beyond the
current administration

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:29.620
and the tools that are
developed through legislation

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:31.083
are actually utilized?

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:34.270
- Well, it's always a risk in a democracy

00:13:34.270 --> 00:13:36.070
that a policy adopted by one government

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:39.230
will be reversed by the next.

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.197
But I think if you look
at the areas of alignment

00:13:41.197 --> 00:13:46.197
and the areas of distinction
between the parties,

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:47.830
it gives you a little window forward

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:50.270
into where we can create
policy consistency

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:51.103
and where we can't.

00:13:51.103 --> 00:13:52.830
Right now, you see the
division that exists

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:56.053
on mask and vaccine mandates.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:00.540
I don't know that that's
gonna be solved anytime soon,

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:01.980
but there is alignment when it comes

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:03.760
to some of these questions
were just discussing,

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:05.120
like supply chain.

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:07.620
I think there's a growing agreement

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:08.840
inside the Republican party

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.853
that we need to have an
industrial policy as a nation.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:15.390
And that belief sort of comes out

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:16.500
of the experience of COVID,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.950
but it also comes out
of the crisis we've seen

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:22.560
with semiconductors and
other critical items

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.470
for the industrial supply chain.

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:28.330
So the Senate, back in the
summer, maybe late spring,

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:31.220
early summer, passed a bipartisan bill

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:32.180
that for the first time ever

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:34.560
really advanced a U.S. industrial policy.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:37.190
We actually allocated billions of dollars

00:14:37.190 --> 00:14:40.500
to help subsidize
industry in critical areas

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.200
so that we can compete with China.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.330
So I think if we do reevaluate
some of these issues

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:47.480
around supply chain and readiness,

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.860
we can guarantee some consistency

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:51.760
from administration to administration.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:53.783
- Wonderful.

00:14:53.783 --> 00:14:57.970
So you were one of the early supporters

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:02.000
of U.S. engagement and
funding for the Coalition

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.750
for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:05.900
and other similar measures.

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:08.900
And for our sort of audiences,

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:11.279
not everyone would be familiar with it.

00:15:11.279 --> 00:15:15.170
It was this entity created
right around the Ebola outbreak,

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:20.150
2014, 2015 outbreak to
speed up the availability

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.330
of vaccines around the world,

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.070
especially in low-income countries

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:28.280
and have those vaccines
ready for phase three trials

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:31.080
after doing initial development.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.140
For a while, until very recently,

00:15:33.140 --> 00:15:36.563
until the Biden administration,

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:41.470
there were funders of
that entity that raised

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:46.470
from Ethiopia to Norway
and Britain in between,

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:52.560
but the U.S. was
conspicuous by its absence.

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:55.430
I know that you have
been an early supporter

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:58.240
of that initiative and similar measures.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:01.870
Any thoughts on going forward?

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:04.773
How do we look at initiatives like CEPI?

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.560
Or is there anything else that comes out

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.723
of this process that fills that gap?

00:16:10.979 --> 00:16:14.630
- I think this is a fascinating
and really important debate,

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:17.620
how to properly set up
incentive structures

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:20.200
to get safe and effective vaccines

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:23.800
for emerging pathogens
as quickly as possible.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:25.910
I, as you mentioned, have
come to the conclusion

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:28.400
that we need to be invested
in international cooperation.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:32.070
And CEPI is, in my mind,
a place to do that.

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:34.770
The Biden administration has
invested, but not enough.

00:16:35.653 --> 00:16:38.110
The Trump administration
sort of sat on the sidelines,

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:39.840
let others invest in CEPI.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:41.240
The Biden administration has jumped in,

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.134
but not with two feet.

00:16:43.134 --> 00:16:44.650
I think we can do better.

00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:49.380
I don't think that that
eliminates our domestic ability

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:52.543
to pursue our own vaccine pathways.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:55.330
There's some that say,
well, we shouldn't be

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:57.190
in the business of
international cooperation

00:16:57.190 --> 00:17:01.480
because coronavirus was actually a example

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:03.950
of how nation v. nation competition

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:06.100
expedited the development of vaccines.

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:08.640
That everybody knew they
had to do it on their own.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:09.550
There was no guarantee

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:11.000
you were gonna be saved by somebody else.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:12.370
So there was a massive investment,

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:15.510
and you ended up with
European work, U.S. work,

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:18.050
Chinese, Russian work.

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:20.890
I don't think it's an either/or question.

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:22.100
I think it's a both/and.

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:25.550
I think you can both develop
your own domestic capability

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:27.850
to create a vaccine and be robustly

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:31.513
cooperating in space like CEPI.

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:38.480
I'm worried about some
of my colleagues' ability

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.480
to view this as a two
mutually exclusive options.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:46.520
So I continue to press the administration

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:51.100
to both plus up our domestic investment

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:55.280
in vaccine research and make
a big contribution to CEPI.

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.383
I don't think we're
gonna lose if we do both.

00:17:58.240 --> 00:17:59.150
- Yeah.

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:03.610
So you have an interest in place

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:06.993
in our sort of in our
legislative and policy process.

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.960
So in addition to serving
on the Health, Education,

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.530
Labor and Pensions or the
so-called HELP Committee,

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:18.150
you also serve on the
Foreign Relations Committee.

00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:20.060
From a global health perspective

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:24.060
and from a foreign policy perspective,

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:28.407
there's a lot of, I would say, instability

00:18:28.407 --> 00:18:30.640
in the U.S. interaction with China.

00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:33.320
Things are evolving, things are changing.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:36.760
We are certainly not
in the early-2000s era

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.680
in terms of interaction with China.

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:40.500
So specifically, sort of keeping

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:41.930
sort of those perspectives in mind,

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:43.900
health and foreign relations,

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:48.000
how do you see the balance of competition

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:51.023
versus collaboration
with China going forward?

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:57.590
- Well, I think the fight
of the next 50 years

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:01.220
will be between U.S. style democracy

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:03.790
and Chinese style autocracy.

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:06.820
The Chinese believe
that American democracy

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:10.950
and European democracy
is withering on the vine.

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:15.090
They think that COVID is
an example of how democracy

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:17.890
and democratic institutions
have become so sclerotic

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:22.030
that they aren't able to adapt
in order to meet new threats.

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:25.250
And well, coronavirus began in China.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:26.860
They would argue that their system

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:28.760
was much more effective
at stamping it out.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.040
Obviously, at great
cost to civil liberties.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.210
And so we've got a lot
of work to do at home

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:38.100
in order to improve the
state of U.S. democracy.

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:42.220
We have to show that
our democracy can rise

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:44.080
to meet the challenges of today,

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:46.260
which is why the Build Back Better Agenda

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.660
that we're discussing right now,

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.840
asking the very wealthy
to pay their fair share

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.140
to fund investments in
the middle of America,

00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:55.470
I think that's important
to our global competition

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.740
with China, as our efforts to make sure

00:19:57.740 --> 00:20:00.070
that everybody in this
country has the right to vote.

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:04.160
But maybe on the top five list

00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:06.820
of devastating Trump era policies

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:09.400
was the decision to withdraw from the WHO.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:11.100
I mean, I don't know that we will recover

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.210
from that decision during the first term

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:14.430
of the Biden administration,

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:18.260
because China, having become
the biggest player there

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:20.070
in our absence, isn't going to give up

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:22.223
that position of hegemony easily.

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:26.370
They are right now the biggest contributor

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.730
to many UN agencies.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:32.550
The UN feels that China
is a more reliable donor

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.660
often than the United States is.

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:37.420
So as we try to compete with China,

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:41.780
as we try to make sure that
U.S. health regulations,

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:45.280
U.S. and European health standards

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:48.010
become the rules of the road,

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:50.310
we've gotta be present in a big way

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:52.290
in these multilateral bodies.

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:56.260
And so this is a time,
and I'm arguing this

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.540
at the Foreign Relations
Committee for the United States

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.060
to double down on our participation
in these organizations.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:04.100
That's one way that we step up

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:06.223
to the plate on China competition.

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:11.460
- As you know that we have
had cyclical interest,

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:14.410
a boom and bust cycle
of public health funding

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:18.584
in this country, public
health departments,

00:21:18.584 --> 00:21:20.930
we were very fortunate in Connecticut

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:25.490
for our state leadership in
terms of getting the job done

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:27.910
with the pandemic, especially
the immunization program

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:29.030
and so and so forth.

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:31.350
But generally speaking, the capabilities

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:35.100
of the health departments
have been eroded substantially

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:36.990
because of these,
partially because of these

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:39.030
boom and bust cycles.

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:41.860
Are there any, maybe it's too
early to ask that question,

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:45.560
but has there been any
thinking to get out of that?

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:49.770
To use policy leavers
that add predictability

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:52.920
to some of those resources

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.930
that health departments specifically,

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:55.890
but also federal agencies

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:59.280
like the CDC, et cetera, can expect?

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:01.200
- I was just having this
discussion this morning

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.400
in my office with Kathy Silard,

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:08.010
who's the CEO of the Stamford
Hospital Health System.

00:22:08.010 --> 00:22:11.650
She was talking about a
really impactful model

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:15.360
that they created and
disseminated around Stamford

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.370
to try to get vaccines into low-income

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:19.810
and non-English speaking communities.

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:21.760
They have extraordinary success rate.

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:22.990
And to her mind,

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:25.560
and it was a really
important collaboration

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:30.510
between the hospital
public health, the city,

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:33.800
local health nonprofits,

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:36.900
and they were successful in
those low-income neighborhoods

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:39.560
in getting 81% vaccine penetration,

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:42.270
which is really remarkable.

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:45.650
Better than we've seen in
many other like neighborhoods

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:47.300
throughout the state and country.

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:52.680
But that's because we
funded that effort, right?

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:55.940
That's because we decided
to spend billions of dollars

00:22:55.940 --> 00:22:58.360
in relatively flexible funding

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:01.840
so that Stamford Hospital could design,

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:05.620
Yale New Haven could design
public health programs

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:09.280
that empower public health professionals

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:12.360
to spend the time that
was necessary with people

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:15.290
to get them into the necessary program.

00:23:15.290 --> 00:23:18.340
In this case, the program was
simple: a shot in your arm.

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:22.120
In other cases, the programs
are more complicated,

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:24.560
whether it be recovery programs

00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:27.180
or mental health treatment programs.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:28.950
But the tools are the same.

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.730
Public health workers out
there in the community,

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:34.870
working outside of a fee
for service structure

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:36.690
in a coordinated way.

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:39.830
So your question is whether
we've learned lessons here.

00:23:39.830 --> 00:23:41.800
I think the answer is we haven't.

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:44.170
I mean, right now, there's
not the kind of talk

00:23:44.170 --> 00:23:46.910
there should be in replicating
some of the models we use

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:49.790
for vaccine distribution, for addiction,

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:52.563
for mental health, for obesity.

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:56.493
And that's really a missed opportunity.

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:00.150
So I'll continue to make that case.

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:02.290
But I will say, a lot of this has to come

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:03.560
from the grass roots up.

00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:08.080
Right now, there's so
little accountability

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.970
when it comes to public health.

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:12.230
Everybody's doing it.

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:15.020
And we just, from the top-down,
we've designed that system.

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:17.440
But we need some help from
practitioners on the ground

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:19.190
to say, how would we redesign this system?

00:24:19.190 --> 00:24:21.690
Right now, public health
districts are doing it.

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:23.010
City governments are doing it.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:23.980
Hospitals are doing it.

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:25.680
Community health centers are doing it.

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:27.630
Sometimes, they're doing it together.

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:29.950
In some cities, they're
not coordinating at all.

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:33.790
And so when you have such
siloed public health work,

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:36.210
it does make it difficult for the funders,

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:38.590
the federal government,
to decide to come in

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:39.670
and throw more money at it.

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:43.990
So giving us a view as to how we can build

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:47.630
integrated systems might
make the federal funders

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:50.053
more interested in putting
significant money in.

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:54.853
- So just to wrap things
up, moving forward,

00:24:56.450 --> 00:24:59.060
what will you and the
Congress be focusing on

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:00.510
to prepare for the next pandemic?

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:02.980
Something perhaps you
haven't mentioned yet,

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:06.900
or something that you wanna
emphasize as we wrap up?

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.220
- Well, I'd hit the highlights
of our conversation, right?

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:12.830
We've gotta combat health misinformation.

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:15.480
We've gotta rebuild our supply chains.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:18.670
We've gotta continue to try
to do work to de-politicize

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:22.453
the conversation around
healthcare and science.

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:26.030
But to finish, we've gotta understand

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:30.370
that if we're not fighting
pathogens everywhere,

00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:32.370
we're vulnerable here.

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:33.800
So we've gotta make a big commitment

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:35.520
to global public health.

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.810
In this Congress, I
wrote, as you mentioned,

00:25:38.810 --> 00:25:42.990
the first sort of
bipartisan bill rebuilding

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:44.550
our global public health infrastructure,

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:47.640
Jim Risch as a hyper conservative Senator.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:48.590
At the time, the chairman

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:50.290
of the Foreign Relations
Committee from Idaho.

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:52.900
He and I wrote this legislation together.

00:25:52.900 --> 00:25:54.330
A version of it was passed

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:57.130
by the Foreign Relations
Committee earlier this year.

00:25:57.130 --> 00:26:00.823
And we are just badly
misresourced right now.

00:26:01.730 --> 00:26:06.730
We spend 12 to $20 billion a
year in global public health.

00:26:07.140 --> 00:26:09.210
We spend $700 billion a year

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:11.850
on conventional military construction.

00:26:11.850 --> 00:26:16.120
That is not the right ratio,
given the ratio of threats

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:17.360
that are presented to the United States.

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:19.470
So we've gotta rebuild our public,

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:21.410
our international public health platform.

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:25.130
We've gotta spend more money
on it, because if we don't,

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:28.780
we can build up all the
prophylactics back here at home.

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:32.290
As we saw in 2020, there's
no barrier, there's no wall,

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:34.570
there's no travel
restriction that can stop

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:37.713
a deadly and contagious
virus from hitting us hard.

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:43.090
- So thank you for a
wonderful conversation.

00:26:43.090 --> 00:26:45.560
And I must say that I
think we are fortunate

00:26:45.560 --> 00:26:48.670
that we have a Senator who
has his ear to the ground

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:51.737
by things like sort of
walk across Connecticut,

00:26:51.737 --> 00:26:53.073
and we feel fortunate.

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:56.250
I think you might also feel fortunate

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:58.820
that you represent
Connecticut, not California,

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:01.193
because that would have
been a much longer walk.

00:27:02.032 --> 00:27:03.570
- Yeah, and I cheated this year.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:07.610
I normally go east to west,
which is about 110 miles,

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:09.910
but this year, given in part,

00:27:09.910 --> 00:27:12.970
the schedule in Washington,
I went north to south.

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:16.030
So yeah, I have it a lot easier
than most of my colleagues.

00:27:16.030 --> 00:27:16.863
- All right.

00:27:16.863 --> 00:27:19.060
No, thank you, and thank you to those

00:27:19.060 --> 00:27:21.630
who connected to this call.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:25.040
I wanna remind those
who are sort of present,

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.650
our audiences that our
next conversation event

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:30.083
will be with the Dr. Angeli Achrekar,

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:34.480
the acting U.S. Global Aids Coordinator

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.360
and Special Representative
for global health diplomacy.

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:39.340
We look forward to seeing you again soon.

00:27:39.340 --> 00:27:40.640
And thanks again, Senator.

